{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-108003",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14094290/assets/4b08d915d0adf8bf603070aa96bde1edf0cfb392d9be5f9bd4786897a6c943fa.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $2x\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =8+1 \\\\ C & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=9-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x)=0\\,m/s.$ We find the displacement of the particle when instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =9-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
